// tests/unit/lying_suite.vt — reports green, exits red.
//
// A NEGATIVE test of the runner, guarding the EXIT-STATUS half of the verdict
// check — the half no other fixture reaches.
//
// The summary grep is `^# .* ok$`, which asks two questions at once: is there a
// summary, and does it say green. So an ordinarily failing suite is already
// rejected by the grep (its summary ends FAILED), and a panicking or silent one
// has no summary at all. All three are caught without the exit status ever
// being consulted.
//
// What only the exit status can catch is a suite whose report and whose exit
// code DISAGREE. That is not hypothetical: it is what a done() that always
// returned 0 would produce once the tallies were still correct, and it is what
// any hand-rolled `exitWith(0)` at the end of a suite would produce. The suite
// says it passed; the process says otherwise; the runner must believe the worse
// of the two.
//
// This file fakes that state directly rather than breaking the framework to
// produce it: it prints a well-formed green summary of its own and then exits 1.
import { exitWith } from "vyto/dev/test/exit";

fn main() {
    print("# lying_suite");
    print("-- nothing actually ran");
    print("# lying_suite: 1 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped ok");
    exitWith(1);
}
